<br>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">The typical reason that su'd scripts fail is because they don't get </font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">the full environment of an interactive session.</font>
<br><font size=3 face="Times New Roman">&nbsp;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">Here's how I do it :</font>
<br><font size=3 face="Times New Roman">&nbsp;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">OENV=&lt;path to user's env file&gt;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">APPUSER=&lt;username of oracle acct&gt;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">FND_TOP=&lt;path to top of binaries tree&gt;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">uSID=&lt;oracle SID in uppercase&gt;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">/usr/bin/su - $APPUSER -c &quot;. $OENV; \$FND_TOP/bin/start${uSID}mgr&quot;</font>
<br><font size=3 face="Times New Roman">&nbsp;</font>
<br><font size=2 color=blue face="Courier New">The important bit is the &quot;. $OENV;&quot; ahead of the command to execute. This sources (in ksh) the users home environment file, ensuring that the script gets the correct environment.</font>
